---
title: AngularJS library
description: The GateKeeper AngularJS 1.x package — a public SPA client exposed as a provider/service and route-resolve helper.
section: Application libraries
---

`@gatekeeper/angularjs` is the AngularJS 1.x integration for GateKeeper. It wraps the
framework-agnostic `@gatekeeper/spa-oauth` public client in an AngularJS module that provides a
`gatekeeper` provider/service and a `requireAuthenticated` route-resolve helper.

The package source lives at `packages/gatekeeper-angularjs`; a runnable example is at
`frontends/angularjs`.

## Requires a public OAuth client

AngularJS SPAs run in the browser, so they must use a **public** OAuth client:

- `client_type: "public"` — **no client secret**.
- PKCE S256 is the only client protection.
- Tokens live in `sessionStorage` and are exposed to XSS by design.
- Add the app origin to the server's `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`.

Prefer a confidential BFF whenever the framework has a server runtime.

## Register and configure the module

Add the `gatekeeper` module as a dependency, then configure the provider in a `.config()` block:

```js
import angular from 'angular';
import { GATEKEEPER_MODULE } from '@gatekeeper/angularjs';

angular
	.module('app', [GATEKEEPER_MODULE])
	.config([
		'gatekeeperProvider',
		function (gatekeeperProvider) {
			gatekeeperProvider.configure({
				idpBaseUrl: 'http://localhost:8080',
				authUiBaseUrl: 'http://localhost:5173',
				tenantId: '',
				clientId: '',
				redirectUri: window.location.origin + '/',
				scope: 'openid profile email'
			});
		}
	]);
```

Only the public `clientId` is configured — no secret. The service finishes a `?code=` redirect on
construction and pushes state changes into a digest with `$rootScope.$applyAsync`, so bindings stay
current.

## Use the service

```js
angular.module('app').controller('AccountController', [
	'gatekeeper',
	function (gatekeeper) {
		this.gk = gatekeeper;
	}
]);
```

```html
<div ng-controller="AccountController as vm">
	<button ng-if="!vm.gk.isAuthenticated()" ng-click="vm.gk.signIn()">Sign in</button>
	<div ng-if="vm.gk.isAuthenticated()">
		<p>Signed in as {{ vm.gk.user().email }}</p>
		<button ng-click="vm.gk.signOut()">Sign out</button>
	</div>
</div>
```

The service exposes `snapshot`, `isAuthenticated()`, `user()`, `status()`, `error()`,
`expiresAt()`, plus `signIn()`, `signOut()`, `refresh()`, `getAccessToken()`, and
`fetchUserInfo()`. The SDK uses native promises — wrap view updates from `.then()` callbacks in
`$scope.$applyAsync(...)`.

## Guard routes with a resolve

`requireAuthenticated` is a route-`resolve` array. Attach it to routes that must be signed in; it
returns `true` when authenticated, otherwise kicks off `signIn()`:

```js
import { requireAuthenticated } from '@gatekeeper/angularjs';

$routeProvider.when('/account', {
	templateUrl: 'account.html',
	resolve: { auth: requireAuthenticated }
});
```

## Fetch userinfo

```js
gatekeeper.fetchUserInfo().then(function (claims) {
	// GET /oauth/userinfo?tenant_id=... (Bearer)
});
```

## Example app

`frontends/angularjs` is a Vite + AngularJS 1.8 SPA (dev port 5178). Register a public client with
`frontends/angularjs/scripts/register-public-client.sh`, paste the output into `.env`, add
`http://localhost:5178` to `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, then `npm install && npm run dev`.
